WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.291 --> 00:00:02.858
♪ [upbeat music] ♪

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:08.258
Give me one minute,
and I'll show you how BigQuery ML

00:00:08.258 --> 00:00:10.901
can enable you to build
machine learning models quickly.

00:00:10.906 --> 00:00:14.066
BigQuery ML lets you operationalize 
machine learning models

00:00:14.066 --> 00:00:16.899
right within BigQuery,
using standard SQL.

00:00:16.899 --> 00:00:20.899
ML usually requires extensive programming
and knowledge of specialized frameworks,

00:00:20.899 --> 00:00:24.629
but BigQuery ML empowers you
to use your existing SQL skills

00:00:24.629 --> 00:00:27.499
to build and use machine learning models
in under 10 minutes,

00:00:27.499 --> 00:00:31.369
saving you from exporting data
and managing ML infrastructure.

00:00:31.369 --> 00:00:34.109
It supports running models
on petabyte scale data

00:00:34.109 --> 00:00:35.639
in a fraction of the time.

00:00:35.639 --> 00:00:38.739
It can also handle preprocessing
like splitting data,

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:41.279
one-hot encoding, and normalization.

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:44.271
And it comes with support
from many types of ML models

00:00:44.271 --> 00:00:45.671
and so much more.

00:00:45.671 --> 00:00:48.851
You can even use AutoML tables
to create best-in-class models

00:00:48.851 --> 00:00:51.789
while handling feature engineering
and model selection.

00:00:51.789 --> 00:00:54.569
To set up BigQuery ML,
create the dataset in BigQuery

00:00:54.569 --> 00:00:57.979
and just write a simple SQL query
with the <i>create model</i> clause

00:00:57.979 --> 00:00:59.522
to create the model.

00:00:59.522 --> 00:01:01.562
Execute the query to run
and observe the model

00:01:01.562 --> 00:01:03.902
as it's being trained
in the <i>Model Stats</i> tab.

00:01:03.902 --> 00:01:06.352
From there, you can use your trained model
to make predictions,

00:01:06.352 --> 00:01:09.002
using the <i>ML Predict</i> function
in a new query,

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:11.262
and enjoy the results directly
in the BigQuery console.

00:01:11.262 --> 00:01:12.532
Just like that.

00:01:12.532 --> 00:01:15.222
BIgQuery ML democratizes
predictive analytics

00:01:15.222 --> 00:01:18.043
and makes it easier for anyone
to get started with ML.

00:01:18.043 --> 00:01:21.311
It's being used for anomaly detection,
customer segmentation,

00:01:21.311 --> 00:01:24.521
product recommendations,
predictive forecasting, and more.

00:01:24.521 --> 00:01:27.531
So, next time you want to build models
using your BigQuery data,

00:01:27.531 --> 00:01:29.731
take a minute to look at BigQuery ML.

00:01:29.731 --> 00:01:33.801
To learn more check out 
<i>cloud.google.com/BigQuery-ML</i>

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.275
♪ [music] ♪

